 The issue with your code is related to JavaScript's closure behavior. When you create a new `setTimeout` function inside the loop, it captures the current value of `i`, which is then updated in each iteration before the timeout actually executes. By the time the timeout functions run, `i` has already been incremented multiple times, resulting in all tasks logging the same value (which will be 4 in this case).

To fix this issue, you can create an array of functions outside the loop and then call `setTimeout` with each function from the array. Each function will have its own lexical scope and won't capture the updated value of `i`. Here's the corrected code:

```javascript
function runTasks() {
  const tasks = [];
  for (let i = 1; i <= 3; i++) {
    tasks.push(function () {
      console.log("Task " + i);
    });
  }
  tasks.forEach((task) => setTimeout(task, 1000));
}

runTasks();
```

In this corrected version, the `let` keyword is used instead of `var` to ensure that each iteration has its own unique `i`. We also use an array (`tasks`) to store the functions created inside the loop. Finally, we call `setTimeout` on each function from the array using the `forEach` method. This way, each task logs the correct number when it runs.

 The issue with the code is due to using `==` instead of `.equals()`. In Java, `==` checks for object identity (whether the two objects are exactly the same instance), while `.equals()` compares the values of the objects. Since you're comparing strings here, it's necessary to use `.equals()`. Here's the corrected code:

```java
public class LoginSystem {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String input = new String("password123");
        String correct = "password123";

        if (input.equals(correct)) {
            System.out.println("Access Granted");
        } else {
            System.out.println("Access Denied");
        }
    }
}
```
